sec.t:
लेक हिलियर पश्चिमी ऑस्ट्रेलिया के दक्षिणी तट के पास मिडल आइलैंड पर है। यह लगभग 600 मीटर लंबी है, मोटे तौर पर छह फ़ुटबॉल मैदानों जितनी, और गहरे नीले समुद्र से जंगल और रेत के टीलों की एक पतली पट्टी से अलग है। हवाई जहाज़ से देखो तो लगता है जैसे किसी ने स्ट्रॉबेरी मिल्कशेक गिरा दिया हो।

बहुत-सी गुलाबी झीलों से अलग, लेक हिलियर पूरे साल गुलाबी रहती है, और गिलास में भरने पर भी इसका पानी गुलाबी ही दिखता है। 2015 में इसके डीएनए का अध्ययन करने वाले वैज्ञानिकों ने इसे नमक पसंद करने वाली शैवाल और बैक्टीरिया से भरा पाया, जिनमें *Dunaliella salina* सबसे आम में से थी। पश्चिमी ऑस्ट्रेलिया की एक और गुलाबी झील, हट लैगून, में कंपनियाँ इसी शैवाल की तालाबों में खेती करती हैं। वे इसका बीटा-कैरोटीन निकालकर खाने को रंग देती हैं और विटामिन की गोलियाँ बनाती हैं।

{{story:flamingos-pink}}

भारत किसी भी और देश से ज़्यादा भूजल इस्तेमाल करता है। ज़्यादातर खेतों में जाता है। [[@green-revolution|हरित क्रांति]] के दौरान बिजली के पंपों और सस्ती बिजली की बदौलत किसान गहराई से पानी खींचने लगे, और पैदावार आसमान छूने लगी।

आज भारत की ज़्यादातर [[irrigation|सिंचाई]] और गाँवों का ज़्यादातर पीने का पानी भूजल से आता है। लाखों [[@wells-borewells|बोरवेल]] इसे ऊपर खींचते हैं। पंजाब, हरियाणा और राजस्थान के कुछ हिस्सों में जल-स्तर इतना गिर गया है कि हर कुछ साल में बोरवेल और गहरे खोदने पड़ते हैं। नासा के जुड़वाँ GRACE उपग्रह, जो धरती के [[gravity|गुरुत्वाकर्षण]] में बेहद छोटे बदलाव नापते थे, अंतरिक्ष से यह तक पकड़ पाए कि उत्तर भारत के नीचे से पानी ग़ायब हो रहा है।

{{story:stepwells}}

Strong winds are dangerous, but the biggest threat is usually water. The storm's winds push the sea towards the coast, piling it up into a dome several metres high, called a storm surge. If it arrives at high [[@tides|tide]], it rises even higher and can sweep far inland.

In 2005, Hurricane Katrina's surge broke through the flood walls of [[@new-orleans|New Orleans]] in the USA. In the Bay of Bengal, where the coast is low and flat, storm surges have caused some of the worst disasters in history. In 1970, the Bhola cyclone struck what is now [[@bangladesh|Bangladesh]], and hundreds of thousands of people died, most of them in the surge.

{{story:weather-forecasts}}

The ISS travels at about 28,000 km/h. That is fast enough to go from Mumbai to Delhi in under three minutes. It goes around Earth every 90 minutes, so the crew sees about 16 sunrises and 16 sunsets every day.

Everything on board floats, but not because [[gravity|gravity]] has disappeared. At 400 km up, gravity is still about 90 per cent as strong as on the ground. The station and everyone inside are falling towards Earth all the time. They are also moving sideways so quickly that the ground keeps curving away beneath them. Scientists call this free fall, or [[microgravity|microgravity]].

{{story:gravity-fall}}

Some animals [[@octopus-colour|change colour as they move]]. Octopuses, squid and [[@power-cuttlefish|cuttlefish]] have thousands of tiny colour sacs in their skin, each opened and closed by muscles. An [[@octopus-smart|octopus]] can change its colour, pattern and even the bumpiness of its skin in under a second.

Other animals change with the seasons. The Arctic fox and the snowshoe hare turn white in winter and brown in summer. High in the Himalaya, the snow leopard's smoky grey coat with dark rosettes blends into rock and snow all year.

{{story:octopus-smart}}

Before plastic toothbrushes reached villages, many Indians cleaned their teeth with a fresh neem twig, called a datun. You chew one end until it frays into bristles, then scrub. Some studies suggest neem may help fight the germs that cause gum disease, though it doesn't replace brushing with [[@made-toothpaste|toothpaste]].

At festivals like Ugadi and Gudi Padwa, which mark the [[@new-year|new year]] in parts of India, families eat neem leaves or flowers mixed with sweet jaggery. The bitter and the sweet together stand for the sorrows and joys of the year ahead.

{{story:toothbrush}}

For centuries, artists or their assistants made paint by hand. They ground pigments with a stone on a slab, then mixed them with egg yolk, oil or gum. Some colours were very costly. Ultramarine blue, made from lapis lazuli, could cost more than gold.

In the 1800s, chemists began making new pigments in laboratories. Bright synthetic blues, greens, purples and reds appeared and were much cheaper. In 1841, the American painter John Goffe Rand invented the squeezable metal paint tube. Painters could now carry paint outdoors easily, which helped the [[@impressionism|Impressionists]] in France paint landscapes on the spot.

{{story:cave-art}}

म्यूज़िकल में किरदार तब गाने लगते हैं जब उनकी भावनाएँ आम बातचीत में समा नहीं पातीं। कोई गाना दिखा सकता है कि हीरो घर छोड़ने का फ़ैसला कर रहा है, दो लोगों को प्यार हो रहा है या खलनायक कोई चाल सोच रहा है।

शुरुआती अमेरिकी म्यूज़िकल ज़्यादातर गानों, चुटकुलों और नाच की एक माला होते थे, जिनमें कहानी नाम भर की होती थी। 1927 में *शो बोट* से यह बदला, जिसने मिसिसिपी नदी पर ज़िंदगी की एक गंभीर कहानी सुनाई। 1943 में रिचर्ड रॉजर्स और ऑस्कर हैमरस्टाइन का *ओक्लाहोमा!* और आगे गया, उसने हर गाने और हर नाच से कहानी आगे बढ़ाई। उसके बाद आए बहुत-से म्यूज़िकल उसी राह पर चले।

{{story:shakespeare}}

रवींद्र संगीत कई परंपराओं से कुछ न कुछ लेता है। कुछ गीत शास्त्रीय [[@carnatic-hindustani|रागों]] पर बने हैं, हालाँकि टैगोर ने [[raga|राग]] के नियमों को खुलकर मोड़ा। कुछ गीत [[@baul|बाउलों]] के लोकगीतों से प्रेरित हैं। बाउल घुमंतू गायक हैं, जिनके टैगोर बड़े प्रशंसक थे। कुछ गीतों में कीर्तन की ताल है तो कुछ में पश्चिमी धुनें।

जवानी में इंग्लैंड गए तो उन्होंने स्कॉटलैंड और आयरलैंड के गीत सुने और वे उन्हें भा गए। पुरानी दोस्ती पर लिखा गीत "पुरानो सेई दिनेर कथा" "ऑल्ड लैंग साइन" की धुन पर है। इतनी मिलावट के बावजूद उनके गीतों का अपना अलग रंग है, और बांग्ला श्रोता कुछ ही सुरों में पहचान लेते हैं कि यह रवींद्र संगीत है।

{{story:tagore}}

1938 में रवि ने नृत्य छोड़ दिया और अलाउद्दीन ख़ान से सितार सीखने लगे। अलाउद्दीन ख़ान महान संगीतकार थे, जो मध्य भारत के छोटे-से कस्बे मैहर में रहते थे, और अपनी सख़्ती के लिए मशहूर थे।

रवि ने लगभग सात साल तक रोज़ कई-कई घंटे रियाज़ किया। उन्होंने [[raga|रागों]] और [[rhythm|ताल]] के पुराने नियम सीखे। राग सुरों के ऐसे परिवार हैं जिनका हर एक का अपना मिज़ाज होता है, जैसा [[@raga-time|राग और समय]] में बताया गया है।

{{story:satyajit-ray}}

People have shared risk for thousands of years. The [[@hammurabi|Code of Hammurabi]], carved in Babylon around 1750 BCE, included rules for merchants who borrowed money for trading trips. If robbers took the goods, the merchant might not have to repay the loan.

In the 1600s, ships carried valuable cargo across dangerous seas. Merchants and ship owners in [[@london|London]] gathered at a coffee house run by Edward Lloyd to hear the latest shipping news. Some agreed to share the losses if a ship sank, in return for a fee. That coffee house grew into Lloyd's of London, which still insures ships, planes and much more.

{{story:probability}}

In 1890, two Boston lawyers, Samuel Warren and Louis Brandeis, wrote an essay called "The Right to Privacy". New, cheap cameras meant anyone could be photographed without permission, and newspapers were printing gossip about private lives. They argued that the law should protect "the right to be let alone".

Brandeis later became a judge on the US Supreme Court. The [[@us-constitution|US Constitution]] doesn't use the word privacy, but its Fourth Amendment protects people against unreasonable searches of their homes and belongings, and courts built on that idea.

{{story:anne-frank}}

In 1947, India was a jumble. There were provinces ruled directly by the British, and more than 560 [[@princely-states|princely states]] ruled by maharajas, nawabs and nizams. Some were as large as a European country, and some were just a few villages.

Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel and the civil servant V. P. Menon persuaded almost all of them to join India, sometimes with long negotiation and occasionally with force. By 1950 the country had a new map, but it was drawn mostly along old [[colonial|colonial]] lines, not around the people who lived there.

{{story:sardar-patel}}

One cicada is easy food. It's slow, noisy and about as long as a paper clip. So the cicadas flood the woods. More than a million can crawl out of ground smaller than a football field. Birds, squirrels, snakes and pet dogs gorge until they can't eat another bite, and still the cicadas keep coming.

[[biologist|Biologists]] call this predator satiation: so many prey appear at once that predators simply cannot finish them. A wild [[@bamboo|bamboo]] in Mizoram does something similar. It flowers all together roughly every 48 years, dropping so much seed that rat numbers explode.

{{story:power-dung-beetle}}

In 1859, the French acrobat Jules Léotard swung from one trapeze bar to another in Paris, high above the audience. It was the first flying trapeze act. He wore a tight, stretchy costume so it would not catch on the bars, and the outfit is still called a leotard.

Trapeze artists use the physics of a swing. At the top of each swing they slow down, and that's when they let go. Then [[@gravity-fall|gravity]] pulls them along a curved path towards their partner's hands. A net or safety line is usually waiting below.

{{story:bicycle-balance}}

A video file is enormous compared with text or photos. In the early 2000s, many homes connected to the [[@internet-how|internet]] through phone lines that took minutes to load a single song. Watching a short clip could mean an hour of downloading.

As broadband spread, connections became many times faster. Engineers also improved video compression, ways of shrinking files by storing only what changes between frames. If a person talks in front of a still wall, the wall need not be sent again in every frame.

{{story:internet-how}}

प्राचीन बेबीलोन और चीन में लोग व्यापारिक सौदों पर मुहर की तरह मिट्टी में उँगलियों के निशान दबाते थे, पर 1800 के दशक तक किसी ने इनका वैज्ञानिक इस्तेमाल नहीं किया था। 1858 में बंगाल में विलियम हर्शेल नाम के एक ब्रिटिश अफ़सर ने एक स्थानीय व्यापारी राजाधर कोनाई से कहा कि वे स्याही लगा हाथ एक इकरारनामे पर छाप दें। बाद में हर्शेल ने पाया कि ये निशान बरसों तक वैसे के वैसे रहते हैं।

1880 में [[@tokyo|टोक्यो]] में काम करने वाले स्कॉटलैंड के डॉक्टर हेनरी फ़ॉल्ड्स ने सुझाया कि उँगलियों के निशानों से अपराधी पकड़े जा सकते हैं। कुछ साल बाद फ़्रांसिस गैल्टन ने हज़ारों निशानों का अध्ययन किया और दिखाया कि वे कितने अलग-अलग होते हैं।

{{story:aadhaar}}

For decades, stimulated emission was only theory. In 1960, Theodore Maiman, a physicist at Hughes Research Laboratories in Malibu, California, built the first working laser. He used a small rod of pink ruby, wrapped in a coiled flash lamp like those used in cameras.

On 16 May 1960 it produced pulses of deep red light. At first nobody knew quite what to do with it, and some called it "a solution looking for a problem". It turned out to solve hundreds.

{{story:apollo-moon}}

Niépce worked with Louis Daguerre, who carried on after Niépce died. Daguerre used silver-coated copper plates treated with iodine, then brought out the hidden image with mercury fumes. Exposures took minutes instead of hours. In 1839, the French government bought the method and announced it to the public. Daguerreotypes were sharp and beautiful, but each one was unique and could not be copied.

In England, William Henry Fox Talbot invented a different process. He made a negative on paper, with light and dark reversed, and printed as many positive copies from it as he liked. Almost all film photography for the next 150 years worked on Talbot's idea of negative and print.

{{story:cameras-how}}

In 1950, a team in the United States used ENIAC, one of the first electronic [[@computers-how|computers]], to make a 24-hour forecast. It took about 24 hours to calculate, so it only just kept pace with the weather. Still, it worked better than many expected.

Since then, computers have become millions of times faster. India's weather [[@supercomputers|supercomputers]], such as Pratyush at the Indian Institute of Tropical Meteorology in Pune, run detailed models of the [[@monsoon|monsoon]] and [[cyclone|cyclones]]. [[@imd|The India Meteorological Department]], founded in 1875, issues forecasts and warnings across the country.

{{story:chance-weather}}

The army issued a new rifle, the Enfield, whose paper cartridges had to be bitten open. Rumours spread that they were greased with fat from cows and pigs, which would offend both Hindu and Muslim soldiers.

In March 1857, a [[sepoy|sepoy]] named Mangal Pandey attacked British officers at Barrackpore, near Calcutta, and was hanged. In late April, 85 soldiers at Meerut refused the cartridges, and on 9 May they were put in chains in front of their regiments. The next evening, 10 May, their comrades broke them out, killed British officers and rode through the night to Delhi.

{{story:telegraph}}

About 50,000 years ago, an iron [[@meteorites|meteorite]] about 50 metres across slammed into northern Arizona. It blasted out a bowl more than a kilometre across and about 170 metres deep. Today it is called Meteor Crater.

In the 1960s, scientists studying the [[crater|crater]] found minerals that only form under the huge pressure of an impact. That helped prove that craters like this, and many on the Moon, were made by falling space rocks rather than volcanoes. Apollo astronauts trained nearby before going to the Moon.

{{story:pluto}}

Apples are Himachal's most famous crop, and their story has an unexpected hero. Samuel Stokes, a young American, came to the hills in 1904 to work with people affected by leprosy. He stayed, married a local woman and settled at Kotgarh, near Shimla.

In 1916, he planted Red Delicious [[@apples|apple]] trees brought from the US. They grew well in the cool [[climate|climate]], and he encouraged neighbouring farmers to plant them too. Stokes later joined India's freedom struggle and took the name Satyanand. Today, apple growing supports many thousands of families across the state.

{{story:apples}}

From their capital, Cusco, the Inca ruled lands stretching about 4,000 kilometres along the Andes. They built a road network of about 40,000 kilometres, with rope bridges over gorges and rest houses along the way. Relay runners carried messages and could cover hundreds of kilometres a day, a bit like a [[@roman-roads|Roman road]] system run by sprinters.

The Inca had no known writing. Instead, officials kept records on quipus, cords with knots tied in them. The kind of knot and its position showed numbers, using a base-ten system, an important chapter in the [[@counting-history|history of counting]]. Quipus recorded taxes, harvests and people. Some may also have stored stories, but nobody has been able to read them.

{{story:potatoes}}

The most famous towers are the West and East Mitten Buttes. Seen from the right spot, each looks like a giant mitten, with a thumb sticking up. Near the spring and autumn [[equinox|equinoxes]], the late-afternoon shadow of the West Mitten falls across the valley and climbs up the face of the East Mitten.

Another landmark, the Totem Pole, is a spire about 120 m high but only a few metres wide. Climbing it is banned. The Navajo Nation closed the valley's towers to climbers to protect the rock and places that matter to Navajo people. Even so, the Totem Pole appears in many photographs, standing beside a smaller spire nicknamed the Yei Bi Chei.

{{story:code-talkers}}

One of the oldest famous word puzzles is the Sator square. It is made of five Latin words: SATOR, AREPO, TENET, OPERA, ROTAS. Written as a grid, it reads the same forwards, backwards, up and down.

An example was found scratched into a column at [[@pompeii|Pompeii]], the Roman town buried by a volcano in 79 CE. Others have turned up across the old Roman world. Nobody is completely sure what the square was for. It may have been a charm, a word game or a secret sign.

{{story:symmetry}}

In Roman towns, many poor people lived in small rooms without kitchens. So they ate out. Pompeii had dozens of [[@oddity-ancient-fast-food|thermopolia]], counters open to the street with big clay pots sunk into them. The pots held hot food such as stews, beans and wine.

In 2020, [[archaeologist|archaeologists]] in [[@pompeii|Pompeii]] uncovered a thermopolium painted with ducks, a rooster and a dog. Traces of pork, fish, snails and duck bones were found in its jars. The shop had been serving lunch when Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE.

{{story:pompeii}}
